M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Community-based distribution of contraception in Zaire</article-title><source>Int Fam Plan Perspect</source><year>1993</year><volume>19</volume><fpage>84</fpage><lpage>91</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.2307/2133241</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B10"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Phillips</surname><given-names>JF</given-names></name><name><surname>Greene</surname><given-names>WL</given-names></name><name><surname>Jackson</surname><given-names>EF</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Lessons of community-based distribution of family planning in Africa</article-title><source>Policy Research Division Working Papers</source><year>1999</year><publisher-name>New York: Population Council</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B11"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Phillips</surname><given-names>JF</given-names></name><name><surname>Hossain</surname><given-names>MB</given-names></name><name><surname>Arends-Kuenning</surname><given-names/></name></person-group><article-title>The Long-term demographic role of community-based family planning in rural Bangledesh</article-title><source>Stud Fam Plann</source><year>1996</year><volume>27</volume><fpage>204</fpage><lpage>219</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8875733</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.2307/2137954</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B12"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Routh</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Ashraf</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Stoeckel</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Barkat-e-Khuda</surname><given-names/></name></person-group><article-title>Consequences of the shift from domiciliary distribution to site-based family planning services in Bangladesh</article-title><source>Stud Fam Plann</source><year>2001</year><volume>27</volume><fpage>82</fpage><lpage>89</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B13"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Debpuur</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Phillips</surname><given-names>JF</given-names></name><name><surname>Jackson</surname><given-names>EF</given-names></name><name><surname>Nazzar</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Ngom</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Binka</surname><given-names>FN</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The impact of the Navrongo Project on contraceptive knowledge and use, reproductive preferences, and fertility</article-title><source>Stud Fam Plann</source><year>2002</year><volume>33</volume><fpage>141</fpage><lpage>164</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12132635</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1111/j.1728-4465.2002.00141.x</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B14"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Prata</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Vahidnia</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Potts</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Dries-Daffner</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Revisiting community-based distribution programs: are they still needed?</article-title><source>Contraception</source><year>2005</year><volume>72</volume><fpage>402</fpage><lpage>407</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16307960</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.contraception.2005.06.059</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B15"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Barberis</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Harvey</surname><given-names>PD</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Costs of family planning programmes in fourteen developi